        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Göbekli Tepe</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 9500 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Pre-Pottery <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, described as hunter-gatherers who supplemented their diet with early forms of domesticated cereal; excavated from 1995 by Klaus Schmidt</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A tell 15 m high and 8 ha in extent on a limestone plateau, with circular enclosures of 10 to 30 m in diameter whose walls hold T-shaped limestone pillars, later rectangular rooms, domestic buildings, quarries and rock-cut cisterns</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the village of Örencik in Şanliurfa Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, in the Taş Tepeler, in the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> plain</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">T-shaped pillars among the oldest known megaliths; carved reliefs of foxes, boars, snakes and vultures; the Lion Pillar Building; a rainwater harvesting system; the debate over settling down and farming; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage listing in 2018</span></div>
