    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The earliest exposed structures were built between 9500 and 9000 BCE and the site stayed in use until around 8000 BCE, so it belongs to the first phase of permanent settlement anywhere in the world.</li><li>Its purpose is not settled: Klaus Schmidt saw a ritual centre visited by nomadic bands, while findings published in 2019 point to a settlement with domestic buildings, cereal processing and a water supply.</li><li>Landslides damaged the enclosures during the occupation, and the site was later left, partly farmed, and misread as a graveyard before Schmidt began excavating it in 1995.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Before archaeologists documented it, local people knew the hill in Kurdish as Girê Mirazan, 'Wish Hill', and considered it a sacred place; the Turkish name Göbekli Tepe means 'Potbelly Hill'.</p></aside>
    </section>

